\begin_layout Enumerate
The simple 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

/
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

/1 queueing system uses exponential probability densities to model the inter-arr
ival times and service times.
 The exponential density for inter-arrival times with parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 is given by the expression 
\begin_inset Formula \[
p(t)=\lambda e^{-\lambda t}\]

\end_inset

 For 
\begin_inset Formula $t\geq0$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Sketch this density function.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Show that both the mean and standard deviation of this density are 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{\lambda}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Briefly explain what these two properties of a probability density represent.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
What is meant by the 
\emph on
memoryless
\emph default
 property of the exponential distribution? 
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
In the lecture notes we saw that the average number of customers in the
 system for an 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset

 queue was given by
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle k\right\rangle =\frac{\rho}{1-\rho}\]

\end_inset

show that the variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 is given by 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle k^{2}\right\rangle -\left\langle k\right\rangle ^{2}=\frac{\rho}{\left(1-\rho\right)^{2}}.\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

